  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure remains in control of our weather into tonight. Expect \nsunny skies into the evening hours, with highs in the upper 70s to \nlow 80s along with low humidity. Winds remain light and skies remain \nclear this evening. This will allow for areas of fog to develop in \nthe typical valley locations. Some mid and high level clouds will \narrive from the south overnight. It will be mild, with lows in the \nmid 50s to low 60s.\n\nWednesday features partly to mostly sunny skies across the majority \nof the forecast area. Moisture begins to increase through the day \nfrom south to north. In fact, PWATs reach 2.0 inches by late \nafternoon over NE PA, and dew points rise into the mid/upper 60s. \nModel guidance has still be consistent showing a minima of CAPE and \ninstability over most of the CWA Wednesday afternoon. The higher end \nCAPE looks confined to western NY and southeast PA. With that said, \nwe can't rule out a late day shower or storm, mainly across the \nPoconos, Wyoming Valley and/or Steuben County. Otherwise, it will be \nanother seasonably warm day with highs in the 80s areawide.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMoisture advection increases along the western periphery of the \nBermuda high heading into Thursday. PWATs increase between 1.75 to \n2.0 inches across the region. Surface dew points also rise into the \nlower 70s. Soundings show substantial CAPE, between 1000-2000 J/kg \nor even locally higher. However, with that said, heights will be \nsteady or even subtly rising around the offshore ridge. There will \nbe only very minor perturbations and shortwaves approaching from the \nsouth. Therefore, in collaboration with surrounding NWS offices, \ndecided to cap PoPs at the likely/numerous level (as opposed to \ncategorical/widespread level suggested by the NBM). Coverage of \nshowers and storms is expected to be scattered to perhaps numerous \nbased on the overall setup, lack of strong forcing and the latest \nCAMS as it comes into time range. So overall, do not expect a \nwashout Thursday or Friday. It is forecast to be partly sunny, very \nhumid with occasional showers or storms possible. Any storms that do \nform could be strong, with gusty winds, frequent lightning and \ntorrential downpours. WPC is indicating a marginal risk for \nexcessive rainfall Thursday through Saturday over the area. Deep \nlayer wind shear is expected to remain generally below 25 kts in the \n0-6km layer, this would indicate a better chance for pulse, pop up \nstorms. However, as a weak front and better height falls approach on \nFriday and Saturday, some more organized convection would be possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA zonal or slight northwest flow pattern looks to remain in place \nSunday and early next week. Occasional rounds of showers and storms \nmay arrive from the west-northwest in this regime. Otherwise, \nheights and thicknesses remain elevated, and 850mb temperatures \nremain around +16C through Monday, suggesting continued warm and \nhumid weather conditions. A front looks to approach and potentially \nmove through the area by next Tuesday or Wednesday. This could bring \na bit of a cool down, with close to, or slightly below average \ntemperatures for the middle and end of next week.",
